      "why": "a JS engine puts integer-index keys first, in ascending numeric order, ahead of every string key and regardless of insertion order. So \"sort the keys\" does not fix the byte order: an implementation that sorts into a new object and stringifies it, and one that emits the sorted text directly, both obey rule 2 and produce different bytes. Rule 2 does not say which is right, so the key is refused rather than guessed at. Prefix it (\"k10\") or carry the map as an array of {key, value} pairs.",
